xl-screenshot (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Command-line captures, for scripts and keyboard shortcuts
    (linuxmint discussion #1765): xl-screenshot captures, saves and exits,
    with no window, whether or not the app is running.
    - What: --screens, --screen [N], --area X,Y,W,H (or WxH+X+Y),
      --selection, --window, --focused, --window-id (X11).
    - Where: --file (or a bare FILE) with strftime codes and $w/$h, - for
      standard output, --overwrite, --format, --quality, --clipboard,
      --annotate; without a file, Pictures/xl-Screenshot.
    - Also --delay, --pointer, --shadow; scrot's spellings work too.
    - Real pixels throughout; clear messages and exit codes (0, 1, 2).
    - On Wayland through Cinnamon's service, which also keeps
      --clipboard captures after we exit.
  * --list-screens: in a terminal, each screen shows its number for a few
    seconds (--identify, --no-identify).
  * Include Pointer on X11 without Cinnamon's service: the real pointer,
    as the theme draws it, instead of a stand-in arrow.
  * Screens icon: a portrait monitor's stand keeps its height (#14).
  * Translations of the new text: de, es, fr, pt_BR, ru (drafts).

 -- Wesley Hayslip <xl_cheese@hotmail.com>  Sat, 28 Nov 2026 01:47:40 -0000

xl-screenshot (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Wayland and Cinnamon's screenshot service (#10):
    - Uses Cinnamon's own screenshot service when the desktop has it, on
      X11 and on Wayland alike; otherwise captures through X11 as before.
      On Wayland without it, says so instead of hanging.
    - Selection on Wayland: our own overlay over a picture of the desktop
      (one per monitor), with the size label and Cancel / Capture.
  * Screens and Screen: with more than one monitor, Screens captures every
    monitor and Screen just the one you're on. Screens' icon is drawn from
    the monitors' layout; the buttons follow monitors coming and going.
  * Window capture like Cinnamon's:
    - Cinnamon's own picker where available; elsewhere (Mint 22) a
      look-alike with the theme's tint and the window under the pointer
      lifted. xdotool is no longer needed.
    - Clean rounded corners at the GTK theme's radius, and an Include
      Window Shadow option (Cinnamon's real shadow, or a drawn one on X11).
  * Accurate Selection and Fast Selection (in the menu): Accurate is the
    Selection you know, with handles and Cancel / Capture; Fast captures as
    soon as you let go, showing only the size while you drag.
  * Screen and Screens have no tooltips: their labels say it.
  * Selection (and xl-Annotate's Crop) tinted with the theme's selection
    color, as Cinnamon's picker is.
  * The delay goes up to 11 seconds (Esc can't cancel it on Wayland).
  * Tests: a stand-in for Cinnamon's service, both apps run as real
    Wayland apps under Weston, faked multi-monitor layouts.

 -- Wesley Hayslip <xl_cheese@hotmail.com>  Thu, 26 Nov 2026 14:16:55 +0000

xl-screenshot (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * xl-Annotate:
    - Curved arrows and lines: a selected arrow or line has a middle
      handle that bends it; the arrowhead follows the curve (#3).
    - Ink pen: thicker where drawn slowly, thinner where fast, or
      following a tablet pen's pressure (#7).
    - Resize (width x height, keep proportions, percent) and Rotate &
      Flip (also R, Shift+R, L, Shift+L), next to Crop. Marks follow the
      image and stay editable (#8).
    - Crop: drawn like xl-Screenshot's Selection, with its controls in a
      popover by the Crop button: X and Y position, width and height (#2).
    - Open, Save, Save As and Copy are in the header bar; the tool bar is
      about 160 px narrower. Opened from xl-Screenshot, just Done (#6).
    - See-through rectangles and ellipses: no darker band where the line
      meets the fill.
  * xl-Screenshot:
    - The Selection panel shows the selection's size in real pixels.
    - On first use, it offers to stay in the system tray (#5).
    - Selection, Screen and pointer captures work on HiDPI screens at 200%
      scaling.
  * Both: US spelling (#4); a Russian translation (draft) (#1); depends on
    librsvg2-common, which draws the SVG icons; automatic tests
    (make check), run on GitHub for every change.

 -- Wesley Hayslip <xl_cheese@hotmail.com>  Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:01:20 +0000

